  "_source": {
    "sample": "Advanced Analytics",
    "origin": "Public Microsoft 'Advanced Analytics' Project sample (.mpp), a companion file for a published book, downloaded from the O'Reilly resources mirror of that book's materials.",
    "export_method": "Extracted with MPXJ (https://www.mpxj.org/), not by running MS Project ourselves. Task graph, durations, calendar and the Start/Finish dates MS Project computed and saved are reproduced verbatim from that extraction.",
    "calendar": "Standard: Mon-Fri, 08:00-12:00/13:00-17:00, zero calendar exceptions (no holidays). The engine schedules in whole working days, so the lunch break is not separately modeled (hours_per_day is reserved-but-inert, see Calendar docstring).",
    "project_start": "2017-08-16T08:00",
    "notes": "The source .mpp has 34 top-level tasks (ID > 0); the issue that filed this fixture (#4323) estimated 35 from eyeballing the sample before extraction. Five of the 34 (ids 2, 12, 19, 26, 31) are SUM (summary) rows whose Start/Finish are a rolled-up display span, not an independently schedulable leaf task; schedule() takes only leaf tasks as explicit input (see SummaryExpansion in engine.py), and none of the kept leaf tasks' FS predecessors point at a summary id — the chain already skips them in the source data. So this fixture holds the 29 remaining leaf tasks (19 ordinary tasks + 10 zero-duration milestones) and omits the 5 SUM rows entirely, which is a representation decision, not a data change: every kept row's Start/Finish is exactly what MS Project saved. All links are FS with zero lag. Verified #4323: schedule() reproduces every one of these 29 Start/Finish pairs at day level, including the milestone-follows-work display rule (10/10 milestones shown at the end of their predecessor's finish day, milestone_at_day_end=True)."
  },
